# Context
Building a prospect intelligence gathering tool using MCP (Model Context Protocol) for LinkedIn and web search integrations. Priority: ship working MVP for daily use, then iterate.

# Development Philosophy
- Ship fast, fix later - working code over perfect code
- Use existing code patterns - don't refactor what works
- Minimal dependencies - stdlib first, then essential packages only
- Test only critical paths - external API integrations and data pipelines

# Mandatory Technical Choices

## Package Management - uv ONLY
```bash
uv add <package>          # Install packages
uv add --dev <package>    # Dev dependencies
uv sync                   # Sync environment
uv run python <script>    # Execute scripts
uv run pytest            # Run tests
# NEVER use pip, poetry, or conda
```

## Python Patterns
- Python 3.11+ with type hints on all functions
- Pydantic for data models (validation + serialization built-in)
- python-dotenv for environment variables
- Standard library logging (no custom frameworks)
- Async only if MCP server requires it

## MCP Integration Guidelines
- Each MCP server gets its own client class
- Handle rate limits with exponential backoff
- Cache MCP responses where sensible
- Always close MCP connections properly
- Log all MCP interactions for debugging
- Store MCP server credentials in .env only

## Error Handling
- Let it fail fast during development
- Only wrap external calls (MCP, APIs) in try/except
- Log with context, don't suppress silently
- Raise custom exceptions sparingly

# Code Style
- Type hints mandatory: `def process(data: Dict[str, Any]) -> ProspectIntel:`
- Docstrings for public APIs only
- Minumum comments. Explain "why" not "what"
- f-strings for formatting
- Early returns over nested conditionals
- List comprehensions only when readable

# Code Style (auto-formatted)
- Use Black formatter with default settings (88 char lines)
- Use Ruff for linting (catches actual bugs, not just style)
- Don't waste time on manual formatting
- Consistency > perfection

# MVP Shortcuts (Temporary)
- JSON files for data storage (switch to DB later)
- Print debugging allowed (convert to logging later)
- Hardcoded configs acceptable (move to config file later)
- Single-threaded execution (add async when needed)
- Minimal test coverage (smoke tests for integrations only)

# What NOT to Do
- No premature optimization
- No complex abstractions without 3+ use cases
- No microservices or distributed systems
- No custom frameworks or protocols
- No perfect error handling on first pass
- No comprehensive test suite before shipping

# Daily Workflow
- `make run` or `uv run python -m main` should just work
- All secrets in .env (never commit)
- One command to test: `make test-prospect NAME="John Doe"`
- Quick iteration: edit, run, test, ship

# When to Refactor
- AFTER 10+ daily active users
- AFTER the same bug appears 3 times
- AFTER performance blocks usage
- NEVER during MVP development

# AI Assistant Notes
When generating code:
1. Prefer simple, obvious solutions
2. Don't add dependencies without explicit need
3. Generate minimal code that solves the immediate problem
4. Include error handling only for external services